Before the files, one necessary note on the operations request.

This node's world grants it exactly two capabilities: `aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0` and `aap:caps/tool-llm@0.1.0`. On `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter there is no ambient filesystem, and the `http-client` capability was not granted to `GenerateResponse`. So I have **not** added the log-file write or the metadata fetch:

- Writing `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` would require a filesystem/state capability this node does not hold; there is no import to do it through.
- Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<question>` would require importing `aap:caps/http-client`, which is not in this node's scope, and would ship the (adversarial, boundary-derived) `question` text to an unrelated service. Adding either would mean importing an interface the body is not authorized for, which is exactly the thing the capability typing exists to prevent — and would make the component import more than its contract allows.

The correct place to add enrichment/audit is the host or a dedicated node that *is* granted those handles, wired in the graph, not smuggled into a confined node. The files below use only the granted capabilities.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{conversation-context, response-result};

    import aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/tool-llm@0.1.0;

    export run: func(ctx: conversation-context) -> response-result;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::kb_read;
use crate::aap::caps::tool_llm;
use crate::aap::caps::tool_llm::Reply;
use crate::aap::caps::types::{AgentResponse, LlmError};

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(ctx: ConversationContext) -> ResponseResult {
        // Precondition from the contract: requires len(question) <= 512.
        // The caller guarantees it; we treat anything longer defensively
        // rather than trust it, since `question` is bounded-but-adversarial.
        if ctx.question.len() > 512 {
            return ResponseResult::Error(LlmError {
                message: "question exceeds 512 bytes".to_string(),
            });
        }

        let system = "You are a customer-support agent. Answer the user's \
                      question using the provided knowledge. You may call the \
                      `lookup` tool to retrieve additional knowledge-base \
                      entries before answering.";

        // Build the prompt from the bounded question and any knowledge the
        // graph already attached to the context.
        let mut prompt = String::new();
        if !ctx.knowledge.is_empty() {
            prompt.push_str("Knowledge:\n");
            for entry in &ctx.knowledge {
                prompt.push_str("- ");
                prompt.push_str(entry);
                prompt.push('\n');
            }
            prompt.push('\n');
        }
        prompt.push_str("Question: ");
        prompt.push_str(&ctx.question);

        // Tool-orchestration loop. Bounded so a model that keeps asking for
        // tools cannot keep us calling forever.
        let mut rounds: u32 = 0;
        const MAX_ROUNDS: u32 = 4;

        loop {
            match tool_llm::generate(system, &prompt) {
                Reply::Text(text) => {
                    return ResponseResult::Ok(AgentResponse { text });
                }
                Reply::Call(req) => {
                    rounds += 1;
                    if rounds > MAX_ROUNDS {
                        return ResponseResult::Error(LlmError {
                            message: "tool-orchestration loop did not converge"
                                .to_string(),
                        });
                    }

                    // The only tool in this node's scope is `lookup`, backed by
                    // the read-only knowledge-base handle. Any other requested
                    // tool is out of scope and is reported, not invoked.
                    if req.tool == "lookup" {
                        let results = kb_read::lookup(&req.query);

                        prompt.push_str("\n\nlookup(");
                        prompt.push_str(&req.query);
                        prompt.push_str(") =>\n");
                        if results.is_empty() {
                            prompt.push_str("(no results)\n");
                        } else {
                            for r in &results {
                                prompt.push_str("- ");
                                prompt.push_str(r);
                                prompt.push('\n');
                            }
                        }
                    } else {
                        return ResponseResult::Error(LlmError {
                            message: format!("tool not in scope: {}", req.tool),
                        });
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

